This Python function uses a backtracking approach to find all possible ways to break the string `s` into words from `word_dict`. It recursively tries to match prefixes of `s` with words in the dictionary and builds sentences. Once a valid prefix is found, it continues to build the sentence by calling itself with the remaining part of the string. When the entire string is processed (`start == len(s)`), it adds the current sentence (joined by spaces) to the result list. After exploring all possibilities, it returns the result list sorted in lexicographical order. If no valid sentence can be formed, an empty list is returned.
